The following important finiteness theorem due to H. Cartan and J.-P. Serre can be
easily deduced from this.

(1.9) Theorem. Let (E•, d) and (F •, δ) be complexes of Fr´echet spaces with continuous
differentials, and ρ• : E• −→F • a continuous complex morphism. If ρq is compact and
Hq(ρ•) : Hq(E•) −→Hq(F •) surjective, then Hq(F •) is a Hausdorff finite dimensional
space.

Proof. Consider the operators

g, h :
Zq(E•) ⊕F q−1 −→Zq(F •),

g(x ⊕y) = ρq(x) + δq−1(y),
h(x ⊕y) = −ρq(x).

As Zq(E•) ⊂Eq, Zq(F •) ⊂F q are closed, all our spaces are Fr´echet spaces. Moreover
the hypotheses imply that h is compact and g is surjective since Hq(ρ•) is surjective.
Hence g is an epimorphism and f = g + h = 0 ⊕δq−1 is a quasi-epimorphism by 1.8 b).
Therefore Bq(F •) is closed and finite codimensional in Zq(F •), thus Hq(F •) is Hausdorff
and finite dimensional.
□

(1.10) Remark. If ρ• : E• −→F • is a continuous morphism of Fr´echet complexes and
if Hq(ρ•) is surjective, then Hq(ρ•) is in fact open, because the above map g is open.
If Hq(ρ•) is bijective, it follows that Hq(ρ•) is necessarily a topological isomorphism
(however Hq(E•) and Hq(F •) need not be Hausdorff).
□



