@@ -0,0 +1,72 @@
+---
+id: lem-qftx-gaga-schwartz-compact-perturbation
+kind: lemma
+title: Schwartz compact-perturbation theorem for Fréchet spaces
+status: draft
+origin: pipeline
+domain: mathematics
+library: mathematics
+dependency_level: 2
+deps:
+  - def-axiom-of-choice
+  - def-qftx-gaga-frechet-space
+  - lem-qftx-gaga-baire-category-complete-metric-spaces
+  - lem-qftx-gaga-frechet-open-mapping
+dependency_roles:
+  def-axiom-of-choice: mathematical-premise
+  def-qftx-gaga-frechet-space: mathematical-premise
+  lem-qftx-gaga-baire-category-complete-metric-spaces: mathematical-premise
+  lem-qftx-gaga-frechet-open-mapping: mathematical-premise
+justified_by: []
+aliases: []
+proof_strategy: direct
+provenance:
+  statement: literature-derived
+  proof: ai-altered
+sources:
+  references:
+    - title: Jean-Pierre Demailly, Complex Analytic and Differential Geometry
+      url: https://www-fourier.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/~demailly/manuscripts/agbook.pdf
+      locator: "Chapter IX §1.B, Theorem 1.8(b), printed pp. 406–407: compact perturbation of a quasi-epimorphism, including its successive-approximation proof."
+    - title: Henri Cartan, Un théorème de finitude
+      url: https://www.numdam.org/item/SHC_1953-1954__6__A17_0.pdf
+      locator: "Exposé 17, pp. 8–10: the compact-operator argument in the finite-dimensional cohomology proof."
+verification:
+  precheck: pending
+page: qftx-gaga-compact-coherent-finiteness
+category: quantum-field-theory-mathematics
+---
+
+## Statement
+
+Assume the full Axiom of Choice. Let $E$ and $F$ be Fréchet spaces over $\mathbb C$. A continuous linear map $g:E\to F$ is called a quasi-epimorphism when its image is closed and has finite codimension in $F$. A continuous linear map $h:E\to F$ is called compact when the image of some neighborhood of $0$ has compact closure in $F$. If $g$ is a quasi-epimorphism and $h$ is compact, then $g+h:E\to F$ is a quasi-epimorphism.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Jean-Pierre Demailly, *Complex Analytic and Differential Geometry*, Chapter IX §1.B](https://www-fourier.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/~demailly/manuscripts/agbook.pdf)
+
+[Henri Cartan, *Un théorème de finitude*, Exposé 17](https://www.numdam.org/item/SHC_1953-1954__6__A17_0.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** Full AC; Fréchet spaces $E,F$; a continuous linear quasi-epimorphism $g:E\to F$; and a compact continuous linear map $h:E\to F$.
+
+[F1] Full AC is the set-choice postulate ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).
+
+[F2] A continuous surjective linear map between Fréchet spaces is open ([[lem-qftx-gaga-frechet-open-mapping]]).
+
+[F3] A Fréchet space is a Hausdorff complete metrizable locally convex vector space ([[def-qftx-gaga-frechet-space]]).
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 First, if a continuous linear map $T:E_0\to F_0$ between Fréchet spaces has finite-codimensional image, then that image is closed and $T$ is open onto its image. Since $\ker T$ is closed, the quotient $E_0/\ker T$ is Hausdorff and locally convex. Its quotient seminorms from a countable defining seminorm family metrize it. It is complete: choose a decreasing absolutely convex neighborhood basis $U_n$ in $E_0$ with $U_{n+1}\subseteq\tfrac18U_n$; from a Cauchy sequence of cosets choose a subsequence whose successive differences lie in the quotient images of $U_n$, lift those differences to $U_n$, and sum. For each defining seminorm, the lifted series has a geometric tail bound, so completeness of $E_0$ gives a limit and the subsequence, hence the original Cauchy sequence, converges. Thus $E_0/\ker T$ is Fréchet. Choose a finite-dimensional algebraic complement $S$ to $T(E_0)$ in $F_0$. The induced map $(E_0/\ker T)\oplus S\to F_0$, $([x],s)\mapsto T(x)+s$, is a continuous linear bijection between Fréchet spaces. By [F2] it is a homeomorphism. The closed subspace $(E_0/\ker T)\oplus\{0\}$ therefore maps to the closed subspace $T(E_0)$, and the quotient map $E_0\to E_0/\ker T$ shows that $T$ is open onto $T(E_0)$. [F2, F3, given, construct]
+
+1.2 Choose a finite-dimensional complement $S$ to the closed finite-codimensional subspace $g(E)$ and let $q:F\to F/S$ be the quotient. The induced $\bar g=qg:E\to F/S$ is a continuous open surjection: $g:E\to g(E)$ is open by step 1.1, and $q|_{g(E)}:g(E)\to F/S$ is a continuous bijection between Fréchet spaces, hence a homeomorphism by [F2]. The induced $\bar h=qh$ is compact. Choose an absolutely convex neighborhood $U$ of $0$ in $E$ such that $K:=\overline{\bar h(U)}$ is compact, and set $V=\bar g(U)$; then $V$ is an open absolutely convex neighborhood of $0$ in $F/S$. Compactness gives $b_1,\ldots,b_N\in K$ with $K\subseteq\bigcup_j(b_j+\tfrac12V)$. Put $S_0=\operatorname{span}\{b_1,\ldots,b_N\}$ and let $p:F/S\to(F/S)/S_0$ be the quotient. Then $p(K)\subseteq\tfrac12p(V)$. [F2, F3, step 1.1, given, construct]
+
+2.1 Write $G=p\bar g$, $H=p\bar h$, and $W=p(V)$. The map $G$ is surjective and $G(U)=W$. For any $y_0\in W$, choose $x_0\in U$ with $Gx_0=y_0$ and set $y_1=y_0-(G+H)x_0=-Hx_0\in p(K)\subseteq\tfrac12W$. Inductively, if $y_n\in2^{1-n}p(K)\subseteq2^{-n}W$, choose $x_n\in2^{-n}U$ with $Gx_n=y_n$; then $y_{n+1}:=y_n-(G+H)x_n=-Hx_n\in2^{-n}p(K)$. Thus $y_n\in2^{1-n}p(K)$ for all $n\ge1$. [F1, step 1.2, construct, algebra]
+
+2.2 It remains to choose these lifts so their series converges. Let $(U_j)_{j\ge1}$ be a decreasing absolutely convex neighborhood basis in $E$ with $U_{j+1}\subseteq\tfrac18U_j$. For fixed $j$, the increasing open sets $G(2^nU_j\cap\tfrac12U)$, $n\ge1$, cover $G(\tfrac12U)=\tfrac12W$: every point of this image has a lift in $\tfrac12U$, and the sets $2^nU_j$ exhaust $E$. Their images are open because $G$ is open. Since the compact set $p(K)$ lies in $\tfrac12W$, compactness gives an integer $N(j)$ such that $p(K)\subseteq G(2^{N(j)}U_j\cap\tfrac12U)$; enlarge the $N(j)$ if needed so they strictly increase. Retain the finitely many lifts $x_n$ with $n\le N(1)$ already chosen in step 2.1; they do not affect convergence of the tail. For each block $N(j)<n\le N(j+1)$, since $y_n\in2^{1-n}p(K)$ choose $k_n\in K$ with $p(k_n)=2^{n-1}y_n$. Choose $z_n\in2^{N(j)}U_j\cap\tfrac12U$ with $Gz_n=p(k_n)$, which is possible by the defining inclusion for $N(j)$, and put $x_n=2^{1-n}z_n$. Then $Gx_n=y_n$ and $x_n\in2^{N(j)+1-n}U_j\cap2^{-n}U$. The sum over this block lies in $2U_j$, because the coefficients $2^{N(j)+1-n}$ sum to less than $2$. The sum of all later block bounds is at most $2\sum_{k\ge1}8^{-k}U_j=\tfrac27U_j\subset U_j$, so tails after sufficiently late blocks lie in any prescribed neighborhood in the basis. The partial sums are Cauchy in $E$; by [F3] they converge to some $x\in E$. [F1, F3, step 2.1, construct]
+
+2.3 The compact set $p(K)$ is bounded in the locally convex space $(F/S)/S_0$: for any neighborhood $Q$ of zero, the dilates $tQ$ ($t>0$) cover the space, so compactness gives a finite subcover of $p(K)$ and, since $Q$ is balanced, one largest dilate contains all of $p(K)$. Therefore $y_n\in2^{1-n}p(K)$ tends to $0$. Continuity of $G+H$ and the residual identity in step 2.1 give $(G+H)x=y_1$. Thus $(G+H)(x_0+x)=y_0$, so $G+H$ maps onto $(F/S)/S_0$. It follows that $F=(g+h)(E)+q^{-1}(S_0)$, where $q^{-1}(S_0)$ is finite-dimensional because $S$ and $S_0$ are finite-dimensional. Thus $(g+h)(E)$ has finite codimension; step 1.1 makes it closed, proving that $g+h$ is a quasi-epimorphism. [F3, step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 2.2, algebra]
+
+3.1 The conclusion holds for every full-AC Fréchet pair and every pair of maps with the stated hypotheses, including the case $g(E)=F$ (then $S=0$) and the case $h=0$ (then one may take $K=\{0\}$). [F1, step 1.2, step 2.3] ∎
