+
+## Statement
+
+Assume full AC. For $0\le r\le3$, let $P^r(\mathbb C)$ be the space of complex lines in $\mathbb C^{r+1}$ with its usual complex-manifold structure, and let $\mathcal F$ be a coherent analytic module over its holomorphic structure sheaf. Write $H^q(P^r(\mathbb C),\mathcal F)$ for the $q$th right-derived global-sections group. Then every such group is finite-dimensional over $\mathbb C$ for every integer $q\ge0$.
+
+For its topology, a coordinate polydisc means the image in a holomorphic chart of a product of open coordinate discs. Take any finite ordered cover $\mathcal U=(U_i)$ by relatively compact coordinate polydiscs such that each nonempty finite intersection $U_I$ has a finite presentation $\mathcal O_{U_I}^{p_1}\to\mathcal O_{U_I}^{p_0}\to\mathcal F|_{U_I}\to0$ whose global section map is surjective, and such that $H^q(U_I,\mathcal F|_{U_I})=0$ for every $q>0$. Give $\mathcal O(U_I)^{p_0}$ compact-convergence topology and $\Gamma(U_I,\mathcal F)$ the induced quotient topology. Set $C^k(\mathcal U,\mathcal F)=\prod_{i_0<\cdots<i_k}\Gamma(U_{i_0}\cap\cdots\cap U_{i_k},\mathcal F)$ with finite-product topology and differential
+$$
+(\delta c)_{i_0\cdots i_{k+1}}=\sum_{\nu=0}^{k+1}(-1)^\nu c_{i_0\cdots\widehat{i_\nu}\cdots i_{k+1}}\big|_{U_{i_0}\cap\cdots\cap U_{i_{k+1}}}.
+$$
+Give the cocycle space its subspace topology and its quotient by coboundaries the Čech quotient topology. These cohomology groups are Hausdorff in that topology, which is independent of the chosen finite cover and local presentations.
+
+## Statement references
+
+[Jean-Pierre Demailly, *Complex Analytic and Differential Geometry*, Chapter IX §§1.B and 4.B–4.D](https://www-fourier.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/~demailly/manuscripts/agbook.pdf)
+
+[Henri Cartan, *Un théorème de finitude*, Exposé 17](https://www.numdam.org/item/SHC_1953-1954__6__A17_0.pdf)
+
+## Facts & Assumptions
+
+**Given:** Full AC; an integer $0\le r\le3$; complex projective space $P^r(\mathbb C)$; and a coherent analytic module $\mathcal F$ on it. Cohomology means ordinary right-derived global-sections cohomology.
+
+[A1] Planned Oka interface: assume AC; on every finite-dimensional complex manifold $M$, $\mathcal O_M$ is coherent, coherent modules are closed under kernels, cokernels and finite extensions, and for every coherent $\mathcal F$, point $x$ and finite $\ell\ge0$ there is a chart neighborhood with an exact partial finite-rank free resolution $\mathcal O^{p_\ell}\to\cdots\to\mathcal O^{p_0}\to\mathcal F\to0$, exact at $\mathcal F$ and at every free term except the leftmost. No global free resolution is asserted ([[thm-qftx-gaga-analytic-oka-coherence]]). Its manifested batch 63 carrier has a ready record that is hash-stale, so uses of it below are conditional.
+
+[A2] Full exact planned local supplier claim: “Assume AC. Let $F$ be coherent analytic on a neighborhood of a point $x$ in $\mathbb C^N$. There is a coordinate polydisc $W$ about $x$ such that for every nonempty coordinate polydisc $D\subset W$ and every $q>0$, $H^q(D,F|_D)=0$. Scalar sheaf cohomology on such $D$ is computed by its smooth Dolbeault resolution. One can choose $W$ subordinate to a partial finite-rank free resolution of $F$ of length exceeding $2N$. The assertion is local in the supplied resolution neighborhood; no untwisted projective Cartan A/B or global Stein generation theorem is asserted.” ([[thm-qftx-gaga-analytic-local-coherent-polydisc-acyclicity]]). The exact planned ID is absent from the batch 65 manifest and has no readiness record. The manifested alternate-ID carrier has a matching stronger statement, but its current Step 1 record is escalated because that record identifies hash-stale readiness for its Oka and bounded-degree-relations inputs. Only the stated positive-degree polydisc vanishing clause is used below; the complete supplier claim remains conditional.
+
+[F1] $P^r(\mathbb C)$ is the quotient of $\mathbb C^{r+1}\setminus\{0\}$ by nonzero complex scaling, with charts $U_i=\{Z_i\ne0\}$ and coordinates $Z_j/Z_i$; it is compact because the unit sphere $S^{2r+1}$ maps continuously and surjectively to it, and the sphere is closed and bounded in $\mathbb R^{2r+2}$ ([[def-complex-projective-space-and-holomorphic-charts]], [[rem-complex-euclidean-space-dictionary]], [[thm-euclidean-heine-borel-pseudocompactness-and-extreme-values]]).
+
+[F2] A coherent analytic module is locally of finite type and every finite-rank free relation sheaf is locally of finite type ([[def-qftx-analytic-coherent-module]]).
+
+[F3] On a finite-dimensional complex manifold, the coherent-section quotient topology is Fréchet and independent of finite presentation and chart whenever the section maps are surjective ([[lem-qftx-gaga-coherent-section-topology-well-defined]]); the Čech cochain and quotient topologies are as specified in the Definition ([[def-qftx-gaga-coherent-section-topology]]).
+
+[F4] On a nonempty finite intersection of relatively compact coordinate polydiscs, a coherent module is acyclic if it admits a length-$2r$ local free resolution on a participating polydisc ([[lem-qftx-gaga-pseudoconvex-intersection-acyclicity]]). The proof uses ordinary cohomological dimension $2r$ on open chart subsets, not compact-support cohomology.
+
+[F5] The restriction map on coherent sections from a chart-open set to a relatively compact open subset is compact when the finite-presentation section maps are surjective ([[lem-qftx-gaga-coherent-section-restriction-compact]]).
+
+[F6] A compact cochain map between Fréchet complexes inducing a surjection on cohomology has Hausdorff finite-dimensional target cohomology; any cohomology map induced by a continuous cochain map that is surjective is open ([[lem-qftx-gaga-frechet-complex-compact-finiteness]]).
+
+[F7] If every nonempty finite intersection in an open cover is acyclic for a sheaf, the fixed-cover Čech cohomology is naturally isomorphic to its derived sheaf cohomology ([[thm-leray-acyclic-cover-theorem]], [[thm-cech-to-sheaf-cohomology-comparison]]).
+
+[F8] Ordered Čech cochains use the alternating restriction differential displayed in the Statement, and that differential squares to zero ([[def-cech-cochain-complex-open-cover]], [[lem-cech-differential-squares-zero]]).
+
+## Proof
+
+1.1 If $r=0$, $P^0(\mathbb C)$ is a point and $\mathcal O_{P^0}=\mathbb C$. Coherence makes its module of global sections finitely generated over $\mathbb C$, hence finite-dimensional. Sheaves on a point are abelian groups and global sections is the identity functor, so positive right-derived global sections vanish. Every finite-presentation quotient topology on this finite-dimensional space is Hausdorff; finite-dimensional complex vector spaces have a unique Hausdorff locally convex topology, so the cover/presentation topology is independent. [F2, given, algebra]
+
+2.1 Suppose $1\le r\le3$ and fix $x\in P^r$. Apply [A1] to $\mathcal F$ at $x$ with length $2r$ to obtain a chart neighborhood $W_0$ with a partial free resolution. Its first relation kernel $K_0$ is coherent by [A1]. Apply [A1] again to $K_0$ at $x$ with length $2r$, and choose a neighborhood $W_1\subseteq W_0$ on which that kernel resolution is defined. Apply [A2] to $\mathcal F$ at $x$ to obtain a polydisc neighborhood $W_2$. Choose concentric coordinate polydiscs $D'_x\Subset D_x$ with $\overline{D_x}\subseteq W_1\cap W_2$. Then $D'_x$ is a neighborhood of $x$. [A1, A2, F2, given, construct]
+
+2.2 The sets $D'_x$ cover $P^r$. Compactness [F1] gives finitely many centers $x_1,\ldots,x_s$ whose smaller polydiscs still cover; set $U_i=D_{x_i}$ and $U'_i=D'_{x_i}$. Each singleton $U_i$ and $U'_i$ is $\mathcal F$-acyclic by [A2]. For every nonempty finite intersection of at least two of the $U_i$, the intersection lemma [F4] applies using the length-$2r$ resolution of $\mathcal F$ on the closure of any participating $U_i$. It also applies to the corresponding intersections of the $U'_i$, since their closures lie inside those same resolution neighborhoods. Thus both finite covers are acyclic for $\mathcal F$. [A1, A2, F1, F4, step 2.1]
+
+2.3 On any nonempty intersection $V=U_I$ or $V=U'_I$, use the first presentation of $\mathcal F$ from a chosen participating $U_{i_0}$. Its relation kernel is $K_0|_V$, and the same local Oka construction in step 2.1 gives a length-$2r$ resolution of $K_0$ on a neighborhood of $\overline{U_{i_0}}$. Applying [F4] to $K_0$ on $V$ gives $H^1(V,K_0)=0$. The long exact sequence of $0\to K_0\to\mathcal O_V^{p_0}\to\mathcal F|_V\to0$ then makes the section map $\Gamma(V,\mathcal O_V^{p_0})\to\Gamma(V,\mathcal F)$ surjective. Therefore [F3] supplies the required Fréchet section and Čech topologies on both covers. [A1, F3, F4, step 2.1, step 2.2]
+
+3.1 The refinement from $\mathcal U=(U_i)$ to $\mathcal U'=(U'_i)$ uses the same index set and sends each index $i$ to itself. In every degree its cochain map is componentwise restriction $\Gamma(U_I,\mathcal F)\to\Gamma(U'_I,\mathcal F)$. It commutes with the Čech differential because restriction of sections commutes with each term of the alternating sum [F8]. [F8, given, construct]
+
+3.2 For every index set $I$, $\overline{U'_I}\subseteq\bigcap_{i\in I}\overline{U'_i}\subseteq U_I$, and this closure is compact because it is a closed subset of the finite intersection of the compact sets $\overline{U'_i}$. Every nonzero component of the cochain map in step 3.1 is therefore a restriction with relatively compact target, hence compact by [F5]. For the finitely many components in a fixed degree, choose in each source a zero-neighborhood whose restriction has compact closure; their finite product is a zero-neighborhood and the product of those compact closures is compact. Thus the full cochain map in that degree is compact. [F5, step 2.1, step 2.2, step 3.1]
+
+3.3 Both covers in step 2.2 are acyclic for $\mathcal F$. By [F7], the induced map on Čech cohomology is the identity comparison through $H^q(P^r,\mathcal F)$, hence an isomorphism in every degree. In particular it is surjective. [F7, step 2.2, step 3.1]
+
+4.1 Each Čech cochain space is a finite product of Fréchet section spaces by [F3], and restrictions are continuous, so the two Čech cochain complexes are Fréchet complexes. By steps 3.2–3.3, their cochain map is compact in degree $q$ and surjective on degree-$q$ cohomology. Apply [F6] to conclude that $H^q(C^\bullet(\mathcal U',\mathcal F))$ is Hausdorff and finite-dimensional. The Leray comparison [F7] transfers both conclusions to $H^q(P^r,\mathcal F)$. If $q$ is at least the number of cover members, the Čech cochain space is zero and the conclusion is immediate as well. [F3, F6, F7, step 3.2, step 3.3]
+
+5.1 To compare two choices of finite admissible cover and presentation, repeat the local construction of step 2.1 at each point and choose nested coordinate polydiscs $D'_x\Subset D_x$ such that $\overline{D_x}$ lies in one member of each parent cover and in the Oka-resolution and polydisc-acyclicity neighborhoods. The smaller polydiscs cover $P^r$, so compactness [F1] gives a finite subcover $\mathcal W$ by such $D'_x$. This is a common refinement; each $\overline{D'_x}$ is compact in the corresponding parent members. The intersection lemma [F4] makes every nonempty finite intersection in $\mathcal W$ acyclic, and the section-surjectivity argument of step 2.3 applies using the local relation-kernel resolution. The section topologies agree under changing presentations by [F3]. [A1, A2, F1, F3, F4, step 2.1, step 2.3]
+
+5.2 For either parent cover, the standard refinement cochain map to $\mathcal W$ sends a component to its restriction with the sign of the permutation that sorts the parent indices, and sends it to zero when two parent indices repeat. The alternating formula [F8] shows it is a cochain map: with distinct parents, deletion commutes with restriction and permutation signs; with a repeated parent, the two faces deleting that repeated pair cancel. Each nonzero component restriction is continuous by [F3] and compact by [F5]; its refined intersection has compact closure in the corresponding parent intersection by step 5.1. The covers are finite, so the finite-product argument of step 3.2 makes both cochain maps compact. Their induced cohomology maps are isomorphisms by [F7], hence surjective and open by [F6]; the maps themselves are continuous as cochain maps, so these bijections are topological isomorphisms. Therefore the two original Čech quotient topologies agree. [F3, F5, F6, F7, F8, step 3.2, step 5.1] ∎
